{
  "draft_approach": "Reframe the site as a linear course application with a dashboard and nine module surfaces: Pre-work, Monday/B0, Monday/P1, Tuesday/P2–P3, Wednesday/P4–P5, Thursday/P6–P7, and Friday/P8. Instead of converting every Markdown file one-for-one, consolidate learner actions into concise module pages and reusable web-native reference pages, while removing operator-only and redundant source documents from the learner path.",
  "key_decisions": [
    "Make the primary graph `/`, `/prework/`, and day indexes with module children such as `/monday/b0/`, `/monday/p1/`, and `/tuesday/p2/`. The dashboard shows current module, required work, completion state, and one unambiguous continue action.",
    "Limit global navigation to Course, Progress, and the five weekdays. Within each day, provide previous/next module controls and a compact module outline; expose resources, handouts, and troubleshooting only beside the task that needs them.",
    "Structure every module around mission, required actions, exact commands, evidence to capture, completion criteria, and optional depth. Move orientation and theory behind short expandable explanations so P1’s six-stage mission remains the visible spine.",
    "Convert source material by learner intent rather than source-file boundaries. Merge repeated setup, evidence, key-command, troubleshooting, and submission material into shared web pages or reusable rendered sections; leave `operator/`, `lead/`, internal authoring notes, and superseded student-pack documents unlinked.",
    "Keep `registry.js` as the authoritative course sequence and extend it with day, module, prerequisite, progress-key, and contextual-resource metadata. Preserve existing local-storage identifiers only when the corresponding learner action remains semantically equivalent; introduce explicit migration mappings for renamed or consolidated checks.",
    "Replace Pre-work’s flat 79-check model with a short required readiness path. Conditional repairs and seven troubleshooting cases should be marked conditional and must never block completion unless activated by a failed readiness check.",
    "Use static HTML, CSS, and JavaScript throughout. Generated handouts may continue using `resources/catalog.json`, but each catalog item needs an HTML landing page and must not appear in primary navigation.",
    "Create static redirect stubs for published legacy HTML URLs, pointing to the closest new module or anchored shared reference. Replace every former `.md` learner link with its web equivalent; preserve meaningful fragments where possible and do not redirect internal/operator documents into learner content.",
    "Validate with a full learner-surface crawl: no reachable Markdown or repository-source links, no orphaned required pages, correct B0–P8 ordering, valid previous/next routes, honest progress totals, exact command-text snapshots, working legacy redirects, keyboard navigation, responsive layouts, and successful serving through both Python static hosting and `server.py`."
  ],
  "alternatives_considered": [
    "Convert every Markdown file into a matching HTML page. Rejected because it preserves the current information architecture, duplicates concepts, and leaves learners navigating authoring boundaries rather than course tasks.",
    "Build one long single-page course. Rejected because progress restoration, contextual linking, browser history, and day-by-day orientation would become fragile, especially for P1 and Pre-work.",
    "Introduce a framework or documentation generator. Rejected because it adds deployment and maintenance complexity without solving the underlying hierarchy problem and conflicts with the deliberately simple static architecture."
  ],
  "edge_cases": [
    "Learners returning with legacy progress that references removed, split, or optional checks.",
    "Direct bookmarks to old pages or fragments, including resource and student-pack URLs.",
    "Commands containing shell-sensitive characters whose rendered text must remain byte-for-byte accurate.",
    "JavaScript-disabled access, where core content and navigation must remain usable.",
    "Conditional troubleshooting branches that become required only after a specific failed check."
  ],
  "risks": [
    "Source documents may disagree about commands, evidence standards, or module numbering and require an explicit canonical-content decision.",
    "Progress migration can over-credit learners if old checks are mapped too broadly.",
    "Consolidation may hide useful nuance unless contextual expansion and cross-linking are carefully curated.",
    "Legacy URL inventory and externally shared bookmarks may be incomplete."
  ]
}